---
/**
 * Grain Studio — support page (required by App Store Connect). Covers iPhone, iPad and Mac.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Seo from '../../components/Seo.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../config';
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Seo slot="head" title="Grain Studio · Support" description="Help for Grain Studio, the film grain and halation app for iPhone, iPad and Mac." ogImage="/og/home.jpg" />
  <article class="column gs">
    <p class="eyebrow">Grain Studio</p>
    <h1>Support</h1>
    <p>Write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}?subject=Grain%20Studio`}>{SITE.email}</a>. Include the device and its OS version, the file type you were processing, and the log: on the Mac, Log → Show log file; on iPhone and iPad, Settings (the gear) → Log → the share button.</p>

    <h2>Requirements</h2>
    <p>iOS 18 or later on an iPhone or iPad with an A-series or M-series chip; macOS 15 or later on Apple silicon. Inputs: HDR or SDR photos in AVIF, HEIC, JPEG XL, HDR PNG, TIFF (8/16/32-bit), JPEG and PNG, and camera RAW through Apple's RAW pipeline. On the phone, pick from your library or from Files; on the Mac, open, drop or pick from Photos.</p>

    <h2>Trial and unlock</h2>
    <p>The download is free. Start the 7-day trial from the licence card (the first screen on the phone; the top of the window on the Mac); everything is on at full quality. After 7 days developing pauses until you unlock — a one-time purchase, no subscription. One purchase for iPhone, iPad and Mac, on the same Apple Account. If you unlocked before and it does not show, use Restore Purchases: on the card, under Settings → Licence on the phone, or in the Grain Studio menu on the Mac.</p>

    <h2>First photo is slow</h2>
    <p>The first photo of a new size builds the grain plates once and caches them in the app's container. On the Mac that takes about a minute on an M-series chip; later photos take 15–30 seconds. On the phone a 12 MP photo takes about 45 seconds the first time and about 5 seconds afterwards; a 50 MP file takes about 80 seconds. Keep the app in front while it develops.</p>

    <h2>Where the files go</h2>
    <p>On iPhone and iPad, finished photos go into a “Grain Studio” album in Photos, HDR intact, and to Files → On My iPhone → Grain Studio → Grain; Share offers the full-size file and the Instagram and RedNote versions. Adding to Photos can be turned off in Settings. On the Mac, files go to Pictures / Grain by default, or to any folder you choose with Change…, and can also be added to the same “Grain Studio” album in Photos. Files are never overwritten: outputs get “-grain”, “-instagram” and “-rednote” names, and a re-run adds “-2”. On the phone nothing in your library is changed or deleted. On the Mac an original AVIF goes to the Trash when you asked for it, and after the full-resolution master was written.</p>

    <h2>HDR looks flat in another app</h2>
    <p>Apple Photos reads the gain map and shows the HDR rendition, and so does Instagram when the file is posted as a feed photo. RedNote gets its own version (1242 px, 3:4). Apps that are not colour-managed show the SDR base, which is encoded in Display P3 like an iPhone photo. If an app shows the image washed out, it is ignoring the colour profile — ask its maker, or export a plain SDR copy from that app. On an iPhone, Low Power Mode and very bright sunlight limit the screen's HDR headroom, so the SDR rendition shows until they pass.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .gs { padding-block: clamp(2rem, 8vh, 5rem); max-width: 42rem; }
  .gs h2 { margin-top: var(--space-7); font-size: 1.15rem; }
  .gs p { margin-top: var(--space-4); line-height: 1.6; }
</style>
